zfsroot (6) blueberry-pie-os; urgency=medium

  * Install on an HFS+ root, and do nothing there.
  * The dpkg path excludes, the data setup hook and apt's extended_states
    move to /etc/dpkg/dpkg.cfg.d/pie-zfs and /etc/apt/apt.conf.d/40pie-zfs.
    The package writes them only on a ZFS root, and pie-root-setup writes
    them at boot. The old /etc/dpkg/dpkg.cfg.d/pie-data goes, and the old
    apt hooks are guarded, before anything is unpacked.
  * pie-data-setup does nothing on a root that is not a ZFS dataset.
  * The apt hooks do nothing once pie-stage is removed.
  * On HFS, pie-seal, pie-unseal and pie-stage commit and run say that
    the root is not a ZFS dataset.

 -- Maxwell <maxwell@blueberrypieos.com>  Sat, 28 Nov 2026 05:10:00 +0000

zfsroot (5) blueberry-pie-os; urgency=medium

  * Data setup scans the package lists in one pass. One native helper makes
    the data directories and sets their owners and modes.
  * Every update of the boot partition marks it first. After an
    interrupted update, the next boot puts the root's boot files back once
    /etc/rc exits, and asks for another reboot.
  * apt updates a read-write root in place only when it is the bootfs.
  * A root left with changes not committed has no known kernel set until
    its set is recorded again.
  * softreboot refuses while the boot partition is marked, when the running
    kernel is not known, and for a root with changes not committed.
  * pie-boot set with the running root drops a stage that is the bootfs or
    read-only.
  * Say that a root has a new kernel or boot files when it needs a reboot.
  * Root snapshots carry the boot session in their names, so that two boots
    with the same clock never pick the same name.
  * Depends on base-files 11. Its pie-boot-partition finds the boot
    partition on a card whose first slice is not the FAT.

 -- Maxwell <maxwell@blueberrypieos.com>  Fri, 27 Nov 2026 20:35:00 +0000

zfsroot (4) blueberry-pie-os; urgency=medium

  * First boot keeps the card's last MiB out of the partition it grows. On
    a card with the GPT that the RG353V's eMMC loader needs, it writes the
    GPT's backup there and points the GPT's header at it. A card with any
    other GPT is left as it is. If an interrupted first boot left that GPT
    damaged, the next one rebuilds it from the backup.

 -- Maxwell <maxwell@blueberrypieos.com>  Wed, 25 Nov 2026 00:51:39 +0000

zfsroot (3) blueberry-pie-os; urgency=medium

  * Build-Depend on pietoolchain-host and pietoolchain-sdk.

 -- Maxwell <maxwell@blueberrypieos.com>  Tue, 24 Nov 2026 17:07:44 +0000

zfsroot (2) blueberry-pie-os; urgency=medium

  * First boot zeroes the end of the partition it grows before it grows
    the pool, so the labels of a pool that once filled the card are not
    taken for the new pool's. A card that had run an earlier pool stopped
    responding at its first boot, and the next boot could not import its
    root. A partition that would grow too little for that end to be
    cleared is left as it is.
  * First boot tries again to grow the partition at the next boot when
    reading or writing the card failed.

 -- Maxwell <maxwell@blueberrypieos.com>  Wed, 18 Nov 2026 16:22:28 +0000

